|Activity|Verdict|Strive for|How not to act|
|---|---|---|---|
|Gratitude|Practice|Name daily what you have; Epictetus called wise the man who rejoices in what he has rather than grieving for what he lacks|Keeping a running list of grievances|
|Rest and leisure|Practice|Real rest: sleep, quiet, conversation, time outdoors; Seneca defended leisure given to thought (On Leisure)|Treating empty time as waste; filling every minute; mistaking scrolling for rest|
|Time in nature and quiet|Welcome, not needed|Enjoy it when it comes, but the sage's retreat is into his own mind, wherever he is; Marcus noted that people seek retreats in the country when they can retire into themselves at any moment (Meditations IV.3). Socrates rarely left the city, where the people were|Needing escape, silence, or scenery to be at peace|
|Criticism and correction|Practice|If the criticism is true, thank them and change; if not, they are the ones mistaken (Epictetus, Handbook 33); Socrates said he would rather be refuted than refute (Gorgias 458a)|Defensiveness; hiding mistakes; the worst case, in Socrates's view, is doing wrong and never being corrected|
|Consumerism and advertising|Avoid|Treat every ad as an impression to test before you assent to it|Buying things to become someone; believing the next purchase will make you whole|
|Meaningless work and burnout|Examine|Ask what your work serves; do it well while you seek work worth giving your life to|Living for the weekend; burning out for things you don't value|
|Eating animals|Open question|Musonius Rufus urged a plain, mostly meatless diet, and Seneca went vegetarian in his youth under his teacher Sotion (Letters 108); the Stoics did not agree, so decide by reason|Deciding by habit alone|$q$, 260, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
