- Morning. Get up when it's time. Prepare for the day by expecting difficult people and choosing in advance how you will act (Meditations II.1, V.1).

- Through the day. Before each choice, ask the six tests, especially better or sweeter and master or slave.

- Evening. Review the day, as Seneca did each night: what fault did I cure, what did I resist, where am I better (On Anger III.36)?

- Weekly. Go without something you take for granted, whether coffee, the phone, or a comfort, to keep it from owning you (Seneca, Letters 18).

- Always. Remember that you are practicing, not perfect. Socrates spent his life examining and improving, and Xenophon counted him happiest because he felt himself growing in goodness (Memorabilia IV.8.6).$q$, 121, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
