Epictetus's rule comes first. He warned against training for display, like a performer walking a tightrope. Train against your own weakness, and do it in secret: to train for thirst on a hot day, take cold water in your mouth, spit it out, and tell no one (Discourses III.12). A feat done to be seen is vanity, not virtue. The goal is not to set records but to reach the point where conditions have no hold on you.

Push any physical feat sensibly. Extended fasting and serious cold exposure carry real medical risks, so bring a doctor in before you go far with either.$q$, 104, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$]::text[], true),
