- Cold without complaint. Socrates wore one cloak through winter and summer and marched barefoot over ice while other soldiers wrapped their feet in felt (Memorabilia I.6.2; Symposium 220a to 220b). Diogenes the Cynic hugged cold statues in winter to train for it (Diogenes Laertius VI.23).

- Hunger with no hold on you. On campaign he bore hunger and toil better than any soldier, and still enjoyed a feast when one came (Symposium 219e to 220a). The feat is a fast done calmly and without complaint, not a test of how long the body lasts; he ate simply and only for need, and never treated his body as something to push to destruction.

- Standing still in thought. At Potidaea he stood from one dawn to the next, absorbed in a problem, then prayed to the sun and walked away (Symposium 220c to 220d). It was not a stunt. It happened because the question mattered more than his comfort.

- Composure under mortal danger. In the rout at Delium he walked calmly off the field while the army fled, so steady that no one dared attack him (Symposium 221a to 221b).

- Fitness kept for use. He fought at Delium in his mid-forties and danced alone in the mornings to stay fit (Xenophon, Symposium 2.17 to 19).

- Voluntary poverty. Seneca advised setting aside a few days to live on the cheapest food and roughest clothes, then asking whether this is what you feared (Letters 18). Cato went barefoot and bareheaded in all weather to harden himself (Plutarch, Cato the Younger 5 to 6).$q$, 258, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$interpretive$q$, $q$unverified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Feats to aim for > Feats of character$q$, 1027, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
