The Stoics who followed Socrates turned his example into rules. This section gathers them, one teacher at a time, starting with Musonius Rufus, the teacher of Epictetus. Every rule below is checked against the source texts. For Epictetus these are the Handbook (Higginson's translation of the Enchiridion, cited by chapter) and the Discourses (Long's translation, cited by book and chapter).$q$, 60, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Rules for living > Musonius Rufus$q$, 1029, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
