Musonius was called the Roman Socrates, and he was the teacher of Epictetus. He wrote nothing; his students recorded his talks. His rules come from those lectures (Cora Lutz's translation, cited by lecture number). More than any other Stoic, he gave rules for ordinary life: food, clothes, furniture, marriage, work, and children.$q$, 52, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Rules for living > Musonius Rufus > Practice$q$, 1030, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
