- Practice beats theory. Would you rather be treated by a doctor who speaks brilliantly about medicine but has never healed anyone, or by one who cannot lecture but heals? It is better to be self-controlled in every act than to be able to explain what self-control is (Lectures V).

- Train body and soul together. You are not a soul alone or a body alone. Train both at once with cold, heat, thirst, hunger, plain rations, hard beds, refusing pleasures, and patience under pain. The body grows hardy and the soul grows brave and self-controlled (Lectures VI).

- Keep the proofs ready. Have at hand the reasons why the things that seem good are not good and the things that seem bad are not bad, and practice acting on them (Lectures VI).

- Build the five habits. Do not love pleasure, do not avoid hardship, do not cling to life, do not fear death, and do not value receiving over giving (Lectures VI).

- Expect to need more training, not less. Students of other arts start with clean minds. Students of virtue were raised among bad habits and must unlearn them first (Lectures VI).

- Look for a few strong reasons, not many. A good doctor helps with a few effective remedies; a good student needs fewer proofs the sharper he is (Lectures I).$q$, 219, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
