- Never sue over an insult. Being reviled, struck, or spat on is no disgrace to the one who suffers it. The disgrace belongs to the one who does it. Bear it quietly (Lectures X).

- Do not bite back. Plotting to return harm for harm is the act of a wild animal. Most wrongs come from ignorance and stop once the person is taught. Forgive, and be a source of good hope to the one who wronged you (Lectures X).

- Remember Socrates and Phocion. Socrates, mocked on stage by Aristophanes, only asked whether he could be of use in another role. Phocion told the man who insulted his wife that his wife had suffered nothing (Lectures X).$q$, 116, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
