- Earn your own living with your own hands. Supplying your own needs is more dignified than asking others for them. Farming is the best work for a philosopher, because the body works and the mind stays free to think (Lectures XI).

- Teach by working. Students learn more by seeing their teacher endure labor in the field than by hearing him lecture in the city (Lectures XI).

- Live, eat, and drink with good men. From good men you learn good; mix with bad ones and you lose what goodness you had (Lectures XI, quoting Theognis).$q$, 94, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
