- Keep sex within marriage. Sex is right only within marriage, and not even there when it is pure pleasure seeking. Adultery is the worst of all (Lectures XII).

- Hold yourself to your spouse's standard. Allow yourself nothing you would find intolerable in your wife. Men should not expect to be less self-controlled than women (Lectures XII).

- What you hide, you confess. No one does these things openly. Trying to cover up an act admits that it is wrong (Lectures XII).

- Marry for character. Do not look for family, wealth, or beauty. Look for a healthy body able to work and a character trained in self-control and justice, in both husband and wife (Lectures XIIIB).

- Share everything in marriage. Husband and wife should hold everything in common, even their bodies, love each other in sickness and in health, and try to outdo each other in devotion (Lectures XIIIA).

- Do not use philosophy as an excuse to stay single. Pythagoras, Socrates, and Crates all married. The home is the city's first defense, and marriage is its foundation (Lectures XIV).

- Welcome children. Musonius argued against avoiding children and praised large families as a good to the city and a duty to the family (Lectures XV).

- Teach daughters philosophy as you teach sons. Women have the same capacity for virtue as men, so they need the same training in it (Lectures III, IV).$q$, 229, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
