- Obey your parents in everything right, and in nothing wrong. Refusing a wrong order is not disobedience. Doing what is right, even unasked, is doing what a good parent truly wants (Lectures XVI).

- Win them over by conduct. If argument fails, be the kind of son who is never contentious or quick to anger, controls his tongue and appetites, and gives up pleasures for his father's sake (Lectures XVI).

- Remember that no one can stop you from thinking rightly. Philosophy is done with the mind, which no one can lock up. You need no worn cloak or long hair to practice it (Lectures XVI).$q$, 104, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
