- Prepare for old age the way you prepare for youth. The best provision for old age is the same as for youth: live by nature, doing and thinking what you ought. Wealth can buy pleasure but not cheerfulness (Lectures XVII).

- Learn to wait for death without fear. Fear of death is what makes old age miserable, yet death is a debt everyone owes, even the best man (Lectures XVII).$q$, 69, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
