- Start self-control at the table. Self-control in eating and drinking is the beginning and foundation of all temperance (Lectures XVIIIA).

- Prefer cheap, plentiful, natural food. Grains and other plants, cheese, and honey; cooked grains and vegetables are fine too (Lectures XVIIIA).

- Eat to live. As Socrates said, most people live to eat. Food is the medicine of life: choose it to nourish the body, not to please the palate (Lectures XVIIIB).

- Know the faults of the table. Eating too much, eating too fast, wallowing in sauces, preferring sweet food to healthy food, serving guests worse food than yourself, and putting off duty in order to eat (Lectures XVIIIB).

- Refuse delicacies even when sick. Zeno, ill, refused the delicate food his doctor ordered and said, "Treat me as you would treat my slave." Once you yield to delicacy, pleasure picks up speed (Lectures XVIIIA).$q$, 143, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
