- Wear clothes like armor. Clothes and shoes are for protecting the body, not for display. The best ones make the body stronger, not softer (Lectures XIX).

- Feel some cold and some heat. Do not bundle up completely. One garment is better than two, and going barefoot, where you can, is better than shoes (Lectures XIX).

- Build a house like a cave. A house should keep out cold, heat, sun, and wind, and store food. Everything beyond that is waste. Spend money on people rather than stones, and gain friends by doing good (Lectures XIX).

- Judge every possession by three tests. How hard is it to get, how useful is it, and how easy is it to keep? The cheap, useful, and easily kept beat the costly and fragile (Lectures XX).

- Prefer sickness to luxury. Sickness harms only the body. Luxury ruins both body and soul (Lectures XX).

- Groom for function, not looks. Cut the hair only to remove what is excess, as you prune a vine, and not to please anyone. Musonius also held that a man should keep his beard (Lectures XXI).$q$, 183, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
