- Know what is up to you. Your opinions, aims, desires, and aversions are yours. Your body, property, reputation, and position are not. Treat what belongs to others as your own and you will be hindered and complaining; keep to what is yours and no one can compel or harm you (Handbook 1).

- Test every troubling impression. Say to it, "You are an impression, not the thing itself." Then ask whether it concerns what is up to you. If it does not, it is nothing to you (Handbook 1).

- Locate the good in the will. The good of a human being is a right disposition of the will toward impressions, and nothing else (Discourses I.8).

- Want nothing that others control. Your master is whoever can give or take what you want. To be free, wish for nothing and shun nothing that depends on others (Handbook 14).

- Aim to be free, not important. Do not wish to be a general or a senator, but to be free; the only road there is disregard for what is not up to you (Handbook 19).$q$, 180, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
  ($q$stoicism-phd$q$, $q$Arete (AI-assisted)$q$, $q$The Virtues of Socrates in Plato and Xenophon$q$, $q$Rules for living > Epictetus > Desire and aversion$q$, 1041, $q$[ARETE SYNTHESIS: an AI-assisted summary written for teaching. Not a primary text and not published scholarship.]
