- Restrain desire for now. Until you are secure in what is yours, desire nothing, and shun only the bad things within your control. Where you must pursue or avoid something, do it lightly and with moderation (Handbook 2).

- Remember what each loved thing is. If you love a cup, remember it is a cup, and you can bear its breaking. When you kiss your wife or child, remember you kiss a mortal (Handbook 3).

- Never say "I have lost it." Say "I have given it back." Hold everything you have the way a traveler holds a room at an inn (Handbook 11).

- Want events as they happen. Do not demand that things happen as you wish; wish them to happen as they do, and you will go on well (Handbook 8).

- Behave as at a banquet. When a dish comes to you, take a modest share. When it passes, do not grab for it. When it has not reached you, do not strain toward it. Treat children, spouse, office, and money the same way (Handbook 15).

- Do not envy. If you want what another has, pay his price: the flattery, the attendance, the praise. If you would not pay it, you kept what he spent (Handbook 19, 25).

- Keep death before your eyes daily. With death and loss in view, you will never think a mean thought or want anything too much (Handbook 21).

- Delay every tempting pleasure. Make it wait. Picture the enjoyment, then the regret after, then the satisfaction of having refused, and choose the victory (Handbook 34).

- Do not wish the impossible. Wanting your family to live forever, or other people to be faultless, is wanting what is not in your power (Handbook 14).$q$, 287, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
