- Measure your duties by your relations. A father is owed care and patience because he is your father, not because he is a good one. If a brother is unjust, keep your own side of the relation just (Handbook 30).

- Learn your duties from your names. You are a human being, a citizen of the world, a son, a father; each name carries its own obligations (Discourses I.9, II.10).

- Play your part well. You are an actor in a play chosen by another. Whether the part is short or long, poor or powerful, your business is to play it well (Handbook 17).

- Serve only with honor intact. Help friends and country in any way that keeps your honesty and self-respect. A faithful, honorable citizen is worth more to a city than money or buildings (Handbook 24).

- Stand by your friend. Do not ask an oracle whether to share a friend's danger. Reason already tells you to stand with him (Handbook 32).

- Say what is right, even at the cost of your life. Helvidius Priscus told the emperor who threatened to kill him, "It is your part to kill; mine to die, but not in fear" (Discourses I.2).

- Count the cost before you begin. Consider what comes before and after any undertaking. Otherwise you will start with enthusiasm and quit in shame, like a child playing at everything and nothing in earnest (Handbook 29).

- Do not take a part beyond your strength. You will fail at it and neglect the part you could have filled (Handbook 37).

- Do right openly. When you have judged that something ought to be done, do not hide it, even if the world misunderstands (Handbook 35).

- Honor God simply. Offer what custom asks, neither stingily nor extravagantly, and follow willingly whatever happens (Handbook 31).$q$, 298, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
