- Blame your judgments, not events. People are disturbed not by things but by their views of things. The beginner blames others, the learner blames himself, and the finished student blames no one (Handbook 5).

- When provoked, buy time. It is not the insult that provokes you but your opinion of it. Gain a moment and you will command yourself more easily (Handbook 20).

- Meet every event with a faculty. Faced with beauty, use self-control; with pain, endurance; with insults, patience (Handbook 10).

- Separate the body from the will. Sickness impedes the body and lameness the leg, but neither impedes the will unless it consents (Handbook 9).

- Take everything by the handle that can be carried. If your brother wrongs you, do not grab the matter by his injustice. Take it by the fact that he is your brother, raised with you (Handbook 43).

- Say "It seemed so to him." Whoever wrongs you acts on what seemed right to him. If he is mistaken, he is the one harmed (Handbook 42).

- Pity wrongdoers; do not hate them. A thief is blind in the faculty that tells good from bad. You would not rage at a blind man. And you would not be angry at the thief if you did not overvalue what he took (Discourses I.18, I.28).

- Judge only what you understand. Do not say a man drinks badly, only that he drinks a lot; you do not know his reasons (Handbook 45).

- Do not measure yourself by possessions. "I am richer, therefore better" does not follow. You are neither your property nor your eloquence (Handbook 44).

- Grieve your own losses as you would another's. When a neighbor's cup breaks you say such things happen. Say the same when it is yours, and when it is something far greater (Handbook 26).

- Do not hand your mind to anyone who insults you. You would be angry if someone handed your body to a stranger. Why hand over your mind (Handbook 28)?

- Guard your mind as you guard your feet. You watch where you step to avoid a nail. Take the same care not to injure your ruling faculty in every action (Handbook 38).

- Never let attention lapse. Keep your principles ready, and do not sleep, rise, eat, drink, or converse without them. No part of life is done better by inattention (Discourses IV.12).

- Make every omen lucky. Whatever happens, it is in your power to draw benefit from it (Handbook 18).$q$, 409, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
